  "result": "I wrote the `drv.motion.left_turn` concept and its three witnesses, and the gate returned **OK** on the first of six runs. All three scenarios produced the expected verdict.\n\n**Concept** (`library/TrafficSpec/Concepts/motion/left_turn.lean`): the body measures the heading change of the road user `x` over the window behind each observation (`prim.heading_change`, left positive). It holds when that change is at least `min_angle` and less than `max_angle`, as the contract says. Measuring only over the trailing window already gives the record's recognition, onset, extent and history rules. A right turn, a drift below `min_angle` and a reversal all fall outside the band. The contract left nothing open, so I declared no narrowings.\n\n**Witnesses** (`library/TrafficSpecWitness/Concepts/motion/left_turn.lean`): the instance uses a 6 s window, a 60° minimum and a 150° maximum. One car drives at 5 m/s and is observed once a second for 14 seconds.\n\n| Scenario | What the car does | Seconds judged | Expected and got |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| holds | drives east, turns left 90° over t = 5–8 s, then drives north; the 6 s change is 67.5°–90° | 7–11 | compliant |\n| fails | drives straight east with zero yaw rate | 6–13 | violated |\n| blind | same as holds, with `kin.yaw_rate` declared absent | 7–11 | unknown, only because of the missing yaw rate |\n\nEach scenario names the seconds it judges because before t = 6 s the 6 s window reaches past the start of the trace.\n\nMy first attempt to search the Core files with `grep` was denied, since the shell may only run the gate command. I read the Core files with the Read tool instead, which didn't affect the result.\n\n`task_result.md` is in the working directory.",
